{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261028T125037Z-2c7ffb84", "ts": "2026-10-28T12:52:22Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "fix/1095-block-append", "build": "ab0798f3951c"}, "body": {"base_commit": "ab0798f3951c04fead19233ba2f94f749cec70e0", "base_tree": "078a02b3dac46fca1df29e7e8b54ed2a15cbf2c7", "head_tree": "08d203ef1569ce41db7128033e17fc9cbc92c6e1", "head_commit": "231d8a36171068aa4133938c76ab09432828bb68", "dispatch_commit": "231d8a36171068aa4133938c76ab09432828bb68", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_host_seam.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "files_changed": [".claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/block-append.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_host_seam.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "files_oracle": [".claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/block-append.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "The clear-reason block does not say 'change the close', so the new answer line tells its turn not to rewrite the close the gate is refusing", "recommendation": "The new STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER says not to write the closing block again unless the turn keeps `RUNNING` / `DO NOT CLEAR` or 'an item tells you to change the close'. The clear-verdict body says that ('Rewrite the close'). The clear-reason body (cmd_stop, the `turn_self_citation` branch) does not. It says to write the handoff note 'then give THAT as the reason', and never uses the word close. A turn blocked only by clear-reason can now obey the opening line literally: it appends a short note with no close. The next Stop reads that note as the last message, finds no `SAFE TO CLEAR`, and clear-reason does not fire. The self-citing `SAFE TO CLEAR` stays visible above as the turn's verdict, which is the outcome the gate exists to prevent. Before this change every blocked turn rewrote its close (22/22 per the fragment), so in practice this gap was closed. Fix: either have the clear-reason body say explicitly to rewrite the close, or have the answer line name the clear gates. Then add a test that every `clear_blockers` body carries the phrase the answer line keys its exception on, so a future clear gate cannot drift out of the exception. The change fragment names only clear-verdict as the 'item says to change it' case.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The answer line's only RUNNING exception is to KEEP one, so a turn whose remedy starts a review is told not to write the new close", "recommendation": "Framework Check 8 (Instruction Clarity): the hook text is an instruction to the model, and it conflicts with session-hygiene.md. STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER says 'Do not repeat your answer or write its closing block again, unless you need to keep a `RUNNING` / `DO NOT CLEAR` close or an item tells you to change the close.' 'Keep' covers only a close that already exists. The most common Stop block is the Critic gate, and its remedy is to run /prawduct:critic. A cumulative/final review dispatches three reviewers and returns at once, so the continuation turn ends with work a clear would kill. session-hygiene.md requires a standing block on 'any turn ... that you end with work outstanding', says 'a live review is `DO NOT CLEAR`', and names 'COMPLETE with a dispatched review unfinished' as a wrong label. Under the new line, the model ends with a short note, and the last verdict the operator sees is the earlier SAFE TO CLEAR / COMPLETE, which is now false. The gates don't catch this: the background_tasks deferral re-arms without any close, so nothing re-blocks to force a correction. The constant's comment justifies the exceptions by what the gates read from the last message, but this case is about what the human reader sees. Fix: word the exception by the property instead of the existing state, e.g. 'unless what you do leaves work running a clear would kill (then close `RUNNING` / `DO NOT CLEAR`) or an item tells you to change the close'. Update assert_block_says_how_to_answer to pin the new wording and re-record the six goldens.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "session-hygiene.md does not cover the blocked-continuation turn that the Stop block now instructs", "recommendation": "Only the hook's block text states the new rule: a turn answering a Stop block does not rewrite the standing block. session-hygiene.md 'The standing block' still says to close any turn that ends a chunk or work cycle with it, and a continuation that writes the reflection after a chunk boundary is arguably that turn. An agent reading the guide instead of the block text gets the opposite instruction. Consider adding one sentence there: after a Stop block, the earlier close stands unless the remedy changes what is running or an item asks for a new close. Then the guide and the hook say the same thing. Prose; this note asks for no edit beyond that sentence.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (no plan claims fix/1095-block-append)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. No build plan claims this branch (manifest scope_unresolved_cause: pointer-claims-other), so the decisions are listed here for the owner to confirm or correct, in the PR description. (1) The answer line has two exceptions where a close is written again: keeping a `RUNNING` / `DO NOT CLEAR` deferral, because the gates read the close from the last message only, and an item that says to change the close (clear-verdict). Without the first, a continuation would fire the deferred session-end gates. The builder found this while building; it was not in #1095 as filed. (2) The unknown-host block gets the same opening as a gate block, through the single owner `_stop_block_opening`. (3) The hook-surface learnings rule now names `bin/_host_seam.py` as the exception to pre-dispatch fail-open (b8bb0223). That is a corpus edit on a fix branch, and the handoff notes record the owner's answer as 'use your judgment'. (4) Whether the fix works is left to the next field survey, not a test. See the separate NOTE.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "The fix's effect has no named instrument: 'measured by the next field survey' names no query", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The tests pin that the line is emitted, which is the right test. Whether models stop writing a second close is left to a survey the repo does not name or commit, and the 22-of-22 baseline has no committed derivation. That makes the before/after comparison hard to repeat. If a repeatable measure is wanted: the continuation turn after a block is a Stop event whose last message the hook already reads, so a fact recording 'continuation re-wrote a standing block' would make the rate a query. This is optional; no edit is required for this change.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1095 (stop-hook: a blocked turn rewrites its whole close instead of appending)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1095 status=shipped closed-by=scope:block-append` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. The change fragment's `closes:` names it, and the handoff notes say one acceptance box is left to the field survey. Also checked: #928 (host adapters), the only item `affecting` plugin/bin/prawduct-hook, is not resolved by this change.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/block-append.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no regression against the loaded rules; amended hook-surface rule is correctly placed", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list (from learnings-files --for-diff): core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, tests.md. No finding reintroduces a pattern these rules warn against. The heading has one owner (`_stop_block_opening`), and a grep confirms no other emitter of 'BLOCKED \u2014 resolve before ending session:' in plugin/. This follows core's 'A review finding names an instance; the defect is a class' rule. The builder's reflection already applies core's 'Enumerate a set by query, never memory' rule to the JSON goldens it missed at first. Rules changed this cycle: the hook-surface pre-dispatch fail-open rule (b8bb0223). It is not a duplicate, it is in the right area file (hook-surface, not core), and it is not methodology content. Its exception matches the code: `_host()` imports `_host_seam` lazily with no fallback.", "files": [".claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: oracle set covered, no bar cleared", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered: .claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md, .prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md, .prawduct/changes/block-append.md. The fragment's claims match the code. `_stop_block_opening` is the only opening for both Stop blocks, a test pins the line on each (gate and unknown host), and both exceptions appear in STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER. The consumer note matches the emitted text. The release-plan row 'block-append | ships' matches the fragment's scope. Nothing ships false, misleads into action, or must stop the merge. record_lint reported zero findings.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/block-append.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md"], "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 6}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "pointer-claims-other", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/1095-block-append`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "ab0798f3951c04fead19233ba2f94f749cec70e0", "record_lint": {"records": [".claude/rules/learnings/hook-surface.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/block-append.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0,